// The two approximate-numeric widths side by side, from the same written literal. type_float is
// 4 bytes (REAL, FLOAT, BINARY_FLOAT) and type_double 8 — except on SQLite and Snowflake, which
// have no 32-bit type at all, so both columns are doubles there.
// What this pins is the FLOAT *family*, in the .json type golden. Before it, no tested schema
// exercised that family at all: the type-check schema had no 32-bit column, and type_double was
// declared DECIMAL although it is binary floating point.
// The width itself is deliberately NOT asserted, because a shared CSV golden cannot carry it: a
// 32-bit float holds about seven significant digits, so its relative error is around 1e-7 — below
// the 1e-5 tolerance the comparison needs for genuine floating-point aggregation. No literal makes
// the difference visible here, and the SQL goldens do not carry column types either. The width
// lives in each dialect's DDL and in its db.json (dialectType: REAL, FLOAT, BINARY_FLOAT) — it is
// declared and documented, but no golden asserts it.

SELECT
 check_type c
  OUT c.type_float single 1
  OUT c.type_double double_precision 2